We need one standard statistical result, which we take on trust.
\begin{beyond}
For $B$ independent Gaussian draws with standard deviation $\sigma$, the sample variance $s_B^2$ has variance $2\sigma^4/(B-1)$. This follows from the fact that $(B-1)s_B^2/\sigma^2$ has a chi-squared distribution with $B-1$ degrees of freedom; the proof is found in statistics texts and is beyond this book. We apply it to bootstrap replicates, which is accurate only when their distribution is close to Gaussian.
\end{beyond}
From this result, the relative standard deviation of $s_B^2$

a sum of many components with random phases tends to look Gaussian (see the box).
\begin{beyond}
The tendency of a sum of many independent random contributions to have a nearly Gaussian distribution is the central limit theorem. We use it here only qualitatively and take it on trust; \cite{rhb} states and discusses it, and full proofs are in probability texts.
\end{beyond}
